]The analysis revealed that if behavioral parameters are not[ taken] into account, the most critical[ parameters] affecting heating consumption are the window U value, window g value, and wall conductivity. When the uncertainty of these parameters increases, the[ impact of the] wall conductivity on heating consumption increases considerably. The most important finding was that when behavioral parameters like thermostat use and ventilation flow rate are added[ to] the analysis[, they] dwarf the importance of the building parameters in relation to the energy consumption. For the thermal comfort (the PM[V] index was used as the established model for measuring indoor thermal comfort) the most influential[ parameters] were found to[ be] metabolic activity and clothing, while the thermostat had a secondary impact.[
